        "reason-2": {
          "type": "reason",
          "settings": {
            "number": "2",
            "title": "What \"BPA-free\" doesn't tell you",
            "body": "<p>\"BPA-free\" only means one specific chemical was removed. It says nothing about whether the plastic itself sheds particles during normal use — that's a separate issue entirely.</p><p>A peer-reviewed 2020 study in <em>Nature Food</em>, from a team at Trinity College Dublin, tested polypropylene infant bottles using standard sterilizing and formula-prep guidelines. It found releases as high as 16.2 million microplastic particles per litre under normal conditions, climbing to 55 million per litre as the water got closer to boiling. In plain terms: heat is what causes plastic to shed — and sterilizing is the exact step meant to keep a bottle safe.</p><p>Source: Li et al., \"Microplastic release from the degradation of polypropylene feeding bottles during infant formula preparation,\" <em>Nature Food</em>, 2020.</p>"
          }
        },
        "image-3": {
          "type": "image",
          "settings": {
            "image": "https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0979/5082/6776/files/5886f11e-42c5-4a4d-8ab9-d4c619785c07_800x800.png?v=1784275037",
            "alt": "",
