        },
        "reason-3": {
          "type": "reason",
          "settings": {
            "number": "3",
            "title": "Most \"anti-colic\" systems are the same old design in a new box",
            "body": "<p>Many well-known anti-colic bottles still rely on the same basic idea: a plastic tube running down the middle to vent swallowed air. It's a real fix for a real problem, but it means more individual parts to sterilise, more joints that have to stay perfectly seated, and more surface area for a leak to start.</p><p>It also means more total plastic in repeated contact with hot milk and hot water — the exact condition that accelerates particle release. And even bottles marketed as \"glass\" often only swap the chamber. The tube, valve, collar and nipple — the parts closest to hot milk and most likely to warp — are frequently still ordinary polypropylene underneath.</p>"
          }
        },
