            "body": "<p>It began with parents near the original Dutch lab, who were given <strong>early access to the first prototypes</strong>.</p><p>Then the same feedback kept appearing.</p><p>Babies who had previously <strong>gagged on long nipples, clicked during feeds, arched away, or pulled off halfway through</strong> were finally staying latched long enough to finish—often within just a few days.</p><p>For mothers trying to protect breastfeeding, that difference matters.</p><p><strong>Less stopping. Less repositioning. Less milk running down the chin</strong> while wondering whether the bottle itself is making feeding harder.</p><p>Moka offered a clearer solution.</p><p>Not another vague <strong>“anti-colic” promise</strong>, but a bottle designed around the <strong>real latch problems parents were already seeing</strong>.</p><p>For <strong>more than 3,618 mothers and counting</strong>, Hosfu became the point where feeding stopped feeling like weeks of trial and error—and finally became something their baby could settle into.</p>"
